  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "d6801519-9acd-44f5-88fb-4ba0623c574e",
      "phaseId": "9a4fa5f5-62a8-4243-85ab-de8edaed21e8",
      "number": 384,
      "shortId": "6RR95",
      "priority": 1,
      "shortName": "a-search-module-in-grid-ui-the",
      "title": "Search as a thing a widget embeds: matcher, frecency, history and the intent handling — one implementation, no widget knows how",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "The whole feature. New module `heca-grid-ui/src/search.rs` (registered in `heca-grid-ui/src/lib.rs` beside `nav`, `keymap`, `focus`). **No filesystem, no clock, and no widget type named anywhere in it.**\n\n**The shape is `ScrollRegion`'s.** `heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/scroll_region.rs:218` owns the paging, the clamp and the axis-decline; a widget that needs scrolling **nests one** and reimplements nothing. Search is the same kind of capability: a widget that needs to be searchable **embeds a `SearchModel`** and reimplements nothing. If any of ranking, history recall or recording is written inside a widget, this task is not done — that is the defect this task exists to prevent, not a stylistic preference.\n\n## 1. The matcher, moved out\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/command_palette.rs:145` — `fn fuzzy(query, text, case_sensitive) -> Option<(i32, Vec<usize>)>` is private to one widget and is the only matcher in the workspace (`grep -rn fuzzy` finds nothing else; `Select` has no filtering, `ContextMenu` has none). Move it verbatim — +5 consecutive, +8 start-of-word, tightness penalty — with a named return:\n\n```rust\npub struct Match { pub score: i32, pub hits: Vec<usize> }\npub fn fuzzy(query: &str, text: &str, case_sensitive: bool) -> Option<Match>;\n/// Case-sensitive only when the query carries an uppercase char.\npub fn smart_case(query: &str) -> bool;\n```\n\n`smart_case` is an inline expression today (`command_palette.rs:275`); naming it means the second search surface cannot spell the rule differently. Its unit tests (`:923`, `:950`) move with it.\n\n## 2. Frecency, counted in uses — not seconds\n\n```rust\npub struct Frecency { /* id -> Use, a monotonic `seq`, a cap */ }\nstruct Use { count: u32, last_seq: u64 }\nimpl Frecency {\n    pub fn record(&mut self, id: &str);   // seq += 1; count += 1; last_seq = seq\n    pub fn boost(&self, id: &str) -> i32; // 0 for an id never chosen\n}\n```\n\n**Recency is distance in the use-sequence, never a timestamp.** No clock in a UI library, no clock skew, no timezone, and a test asserts an exact number instead of sleeping.\n\n`boost = COUNT_WEIGHT * log2(1 + count) + RECENCY_WEIGHT / (1 + (seq - last_seq))`, integer arithmetic, **capped at `BOOST_MAX`**. The cap is the design decision: fuzzy scores are small (a word-start hit is +8), so an uncapped frecency term would let an often-chosen entry outrank one the query actually spells better. Frecency decides ties and near-ties; a clearly better textual match still wins. Keep the three constants together with that reasoning beside them — they will be tuned by eye.\n\nBounded: cap the table (~500 ids), prune the lowest `boost` on overflow.\n\n## 3. History — a ring with a cursor and a draft\n\n```rust\npub struct History { /* entries, cursor, draft, cap */ }\nimpl History {\n    pub fn push(&mut self, query: &str);\n    pub fn older(&mut self, current: &str) -> Option<&str>;\n    pub fn newer(&mut self) -> Option<&str>;\n    pub fn reset(&mut self);\n}\n```\n\n- `push` ignores an empty query and a consecutive repeat of the newest entry.\n- **The draft**: the first `older()` stashes what the user had typed, and walking back down past the newest restores it instead of stranding the field on the last history item. Every shell prompt does this and its absence is felt immediately.\n- Walking past the oldest stops — no wrapping. A wrapped history hides from the user that they have looped.\n- Cap ~50 per scope, oldest dropped first.\n\n## 4. Scope — the `>` / `@` / `:` seam, in from day one\n\n```rust\npub struct SearchStore { /* scope -> (History, Frecency) */ }\n```\n\nOne scope exists now, `\"command\"`. It is keyed from the start **because retrofitting a key into a persisted file means migrating it** — adding `\"symbol\"` for `@` must be data, not a reshape. The store is also the whole snapshot the app persists, so serialization has exactly one subject.\n\n## 5. `SearchModel` — the part a widget embeds\n\nThis is what stops the next widget from reimplementing anything:\n\n```rust\npub struct SearchModel { scope: String, store: Rc<RefCell<SearchStore>>, /* … */ }\n\npub enum SearchAction { SetQuery(String), None }\n\nimpl SearchModel {\n    /// Rank any list. The closure yields `(id, searchable_text)`, so the module\n    /// never names a widget's item type — `Command`, `MenuEntry` and whatever\n    /// comes next all pass through the same call.\n    pub fn rank<T>(&self, items: &[T], key: impl Fn(&T) -> (Option<&str>, &str)) -> Vec<Ranked>;\n\n    /// **The two intents, handled once.** A widget forwards the intent and applies\n    /// what comes back. This is the whole of history navigation; a widget that\n    /// matches `MenuHistoryUp` itself has copied logic that lives here.\n    pub fn handle(&mut self, intent: WidgetIntent, current_query: &str) -> SearchAction;\n\n    /// A keystroke leaves history behind.\n    pub fn query_changed(&mut self);\n\n    /// On a successful run: the query as typed, and the chosen id if it has one.\n    pub fn record_run(&mut self, query: &str, id: Option<&str>);\n}\n```\n\n`rank` taking `(Option<&str>, &str)` is deliberate: **identity is optional and belongs to the caller**. An entry with no id still matches and still sorts, it just carries no boost — so a widget is never forced to grow an id field to become searchable. (The palette *does* have a stable id to give it, and will.)\n\n`Ranked` carries the item index, the total score and the hit indices, so the caller paints its own highlights exactly as the palette does today.\n\n## What this task does NOT decide\n\n**Grouping is the caller's.** The palette's host sorts the focused component's actions ahead of the rest (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:118`, P085's rule) — that is app policy about chrome focus and has no business in a search module. `rank` returns one ordered list by score; a caller that wants groups sorts by `(group, rank_position)` itself. Any other search surface will have different groups, or none.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- An id recorded but never listed again simply never gets its boost applied — no cleanup pass, no dangling reference; the cap prunes it.\n- `boost` on an unknown id is 0, so an empty store reproduces today's ordering **exactly** — that must be recoverable.\n- Multi-byte queries: the matcher counts chars; `History` stores `String` and must never slice by byte.\n- Two widgets sharing one store and one scope share a history — that is intended (the `>` and `@` modes of one palette are separate scopes; two palettes are not).\n\n## Tests (pure — no window, no fs, no widget)\n\n- The matcher's existing tests, moved, still green.\n- `record`/`boost`: chosen 5 times outranks chosen once; a recent single use outranks an old single use.\n- **The cap is asserted directly**: a much-used entry with a poor textual match does *not* overtake a clearly better match. That is the property the constants exist to guarantee.\n- History: dedupe, the stop at the oldest, and the draft returning on the way down.\n- `handle` drives a full walk — up, up, down, down — from intents alone, with no widget in the test. If that test needs a widget, the seam is in the wrong place.\n- An empty `SearchStore` reproduces today's ordering exactly.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone. New module `heca-grid-ui/src/search.rs` — no filesystem, no clock, and no widget type named in it.\n\n- `fuzzy` moved out of `command_palette.rs` with a named `Match { score, hits }`; `smart_case` given a name so the second search surface cannot spell the rule differently. The palette's copy still exists and is removed in T385.\n- `Frecency` — recency is **distance in a use-sequence**, never a timestamp: no clock in a UI library, no skew, and a test asserts an exact number instead of sleeping. `boost = COUNT_WEIGHT·log2(1+count) + RECENCY_WEIGHT/(1+age)`, capped.\n- `History` — a bounded ring with a cursor and the **draft**: the first `older()` stashes what was being typed, and walking back down past the newest restores it. Stops at the oldest rather than wrapping.\n- `SearchStore` / `Scope` — keyed by scope from the first line written, with only `\"command\"` in use, so `>` / `@` / `:` arrive as data rather than a file migration. Exposes `entries`/`seq`/`restore` for T386 to persist.\n- `SearchModel` — the part a widget embeds: `rank` (a closure yields `(Option<id>, text)`, so identity is optional and no widget must grow an id field to be searchable), `handle` (the whole of history navigation, one implementation), `query_changed`, `record_run`.\n- `WidgetIntent::MenuHistoryUp` / `MenuHistoryDown` added to the vocabulary in `component.rs`.\n\n**Calibration lesson, written into the constants.** The first weights (6/12, cap 18) had five-uses-recently and once-just-now *both* sum past the cap, so they scored identically and the ranking said nothing — the cap swallowed the signal it exists to bound. Now 4/6 with a cap of 20: everyday values land around 4–14 and the cap is a wall for outliers only. The count term deliberately outweighs recency — how often you reach for something says more than whether it was the last thing you reached for.\n\nTests are pure: no window, no fs, no widget. The full history walk is driven by intents alone — if that test needed a widget, the seam would be in the wrong place. `an_empty_store_changes_no_ordering` guards that first-run behaviour is identical to no-frecency behaviour, and `frecency_decides_ties_but_never_beats_a_better_match` asserts the property the cap exists for.",
      "notes": "",
