    {
      "id": "8c7b8aba-115d-4bd7-805b-7a82e2ccde8e",
      "phaseId": "9a4fa5f5-62a8-4243-85ab-de8edaed21e8",
      "number": 385,
      "shortId": "QCV4E",
      "priority": 2,
      "shortName": "the-palette-uses-it-command-id",
      "title": "CommandPalette becomes the first consumer — it embeds a SearchModel and implements none of it",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "The palette is where the capability is *first used*, not where it lives. Depends on T384. **Measure of success: nothing here matches, ranks or recalls anything — every one of those calls into `SearchModel`.** If this task grows an `if intent == MenuHistoryUp` arm that walks a history, the seam was built in the wrong place and T384 is not done.\n\n## 1. The palette embeds a model\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/command_palette.rs:180` — the struct gains a `SearchModel`, handed in by the host with its scope (`\"command\"`) and the shared store. The palette is rebuilt on every open (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:185`), so the store cannot live in the widget — same reason the open state is a host-owned `Signal` (`command_palette.rs:~259`).\n\n## 2. Three call sites, all delegating\n\n- **`results()`** (`command_palette.rs:273`) — today it maps the private `fuzzy` and sorts by `Reverse(score)`. It becomes one `model.rank(&self.commands, |c| (c.id.as_deref(), &c.label))` call. Filtering still matches the **label only**, never the description (`command_palette.rs:56`, a deliberate rule) — that is the closure's second field, and the only place that rule is expressed.\n- **`on_event_capture`** (`command_palette.rs:798`, beside the `MenuUp`/`MenuDown` arms) — forwards the intent to `model.handle(...)` and applies the returned `SearchAction`. Applying `SetQuery` means setting the `Input`'s text and calling the existing `on_query_changed()` (`:443`) so `selected`/`scroll` reset. The `_` arm below forwards every unlisted intent to the query `Input`, so the two history intents must be reached before it.\n- **`run_selected()`** (`command_palette.rs:310`) — `model.record_run(&query, id)` on a successful run only. Esc records nothing: an abandoned search is not one the user wants back.\n\n## 3. `Command::id`\n\n`command_palette.rs:30` — `struct Command { label, description, icon, chords, on_run }` has no identity, and the label is not one: the host prefixes it with the owning component's title (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:91` → `\"Workspaces › Delete Row\"`), so it changes with mounting. Add `Command::id(impl Into<String>)` — a scalar, so `#[heca_grid_ui_macros::prop]` and an SDK setter, which `heca-grid-ui/tests/prop_drift.rs` will demand exactly as it did for `Label::truncate` in T379.\n\n**Optional, by design.** `rank` takes `Option<&str>`; a `Command` without an id matches and sorts normally and just carries no boost. The host already has the value and currently discards it — `PaletteEntry.id` (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:43`) reaches the overlay round-trip (`:196`) but not the widget's `Command` (`:200`).\n\n## 4. The intents and their keys — vocabulary, not palette features\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/component.rs:393` `WidgetIntent` gains `MenuHistoryUp` / `MenuHistoryDown`, documented beside `MenuUp`/`MenuDown` (:398-401) as available to **any** search surface. Names follow the table's axis convention: `item_*` is horizontal and uses previous/next, `menu_*` is vertical and uses up/down — and beside `item_previous`, a \"history_prev\" would read ambiguously (an older entry, or one row up?). Up is older, down is newer, matching the arrows.\n\n`keybindings.default.toml:339` `[keys.widgets]`, with the name→intent table at `heca/src/app/registry.rs:266-276`:\n\n```toml\nmenu_history_up   = [\"Shift+ArrowUp\", \"Ctrl+p\"]     # older\nmenu_history_down = [\"Shift+ArrowDown\", \"Ctrl+n\"]   # newer\n```\n\nChecked, not assumed — the first proposal (`Ctrl+j`/`Ctrl+k`, plain arrows) was **already** `menu_down`/`menu_up` (:342-343) for every menu, `Select` and the palette:\n- `Ctrl+p`/`Ctrl+n` exist only as `prefix+Ctrl+p`/`prefix+Ctrl+n` (`workspace_prev`/`next`, :115-116) — prefix-gated `WmAction`s that cannot reach a modal overlay.\n- `Shift+ArrowUp/Down` is unbound; only `prefix+Shift+ArrowLeft/Right` exists (:42-43).\n- The query field passes both through: `Input` returns `Handled::No` for `Char` with ctrl/meta (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/input.rs:317`) and handles only `ArrowLeft`/`ArrowRight`/`Home`/`End` (:322-325), so vertical arrows and `Ctrl+<char>` fall through to the host keymap — the same path `menu_up`/`menu_down` take.\n\nUpdate the `[keys.widgets]` header comment (:325-338), which enumerates the vocabulary, and its mirror in `heca-config/src/keys.rs:186`.\n\n**No `ActionRegistry` entry.** These are `WidgetIntent`s: meaningless outside an open search surface, no `ActionPolicy`, nothing to offer RPC — the same reasons `menu_up`/`menu_down` are not actions. (`clear_search_history` *is* a real action and belongs with the store, T386.)\n\n## 5. Host wiring, and the one ordering rule — **focus decides until the user types**\n\n`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:185` passes the store handle and the scope; the build loop (`:200`) sets `Command::id(row.id)`.\n\nToday `palette.rs:118` unconditionally sorts the focused component's actions ahead of everything else (P085's rule). That becomes **conditional on the query being empty**, settled 2026-08-03:\n\n- **Empty query** — the user is browsing, and the only context available is what they have focused. Keep the focused component's actions on top, ordered inside that block by frecency, with everything else below in frecency order. A component's actions are otherwise buried among a hundred built-ins, and there are only a handful of them, so the block pushes almost nothing down. This is also where a flat most-used list helps least: the actions run fifty times a day are the ones that already have keybindings.\n- **Non-empty query** — the user has said what they want, and the query is better context than whatever happens to hold focus. **Drop the block entirely** and take `rank`'s order as-is (match score + frecency boost). Keeping it here would actively fight the user: an action whose name they spelled correctly must not sit below one they did not, merely because its component has focus.\n\nExpressed as a sort key of `(query.is_empty() && !focused, rank_position)` or equivalent — one place, host-side. **The search module still knows nothing about blocks**: `rank` returns a single ordered list either way, which is what makes this reversible at no cost.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- A recalled query matching nothing leaves an empty list — `select_next` already guards `n == 0` (`command_palette.rs:294`).\n- Recalling a query from history is a **non-empty** query, so the block drops — consistent with typing it by hand, which is what recall is.\n- Deleting back to an empty query brings the block back. That is a visible reshuffle mid-typing; it is the intended behaviour, but it is the first thing to look at if the ordering ever feels unstable.\n- Held Shift while walking history must not smear a text selection through the query line — the field only anchors on `ArrowLeft`/`ArrowRight` (`input.rs:322-325`), so verify rather than assume.\n- Two spellings of one key drew a keycap chip twice once before; `chord_caps` dedupes by cap, so check the palette's hint rows if these bindings ever surface there.\n\n## Tests\n\n- **Assert the drawn query text**, not a field: paint into a `Scene`, dispatch `MenuHistoryUp`, read the query line's `DrawCommand::Text`. Asserting an internal string is the F004 lesson repeated.\n- Walking down past the newest restores the draft.\n- Ordering, both halves of the rule: with an empty query the focused component's actions lead even when a built-in has a far higher frecency; with a query typed, that same built-in leads if it matches better. The existing P085 test (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:326`) asserts the first half and must gain the second — it is currently written as if the block were unconditional.\n- With a store recording one command three times, it leads on an empty query within its own block.\n- Esc records nothing.\n\n**Visual verification is the user's** — the recall itself, whether the reshuffle on emptying the query is jarring in practice, and that Shift+Arrow does not smear a selection through the query line.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone — verified in the app by the user 2026-08-04. The palette embeds a `SearchModel` and implements none of it: `results()` is one `rank` call, the two history intents forward to `handle`, `run_selected` calls `record_run`. Its private `fuzzy` and `Match` are deleted, so the matcher exists once.\n\n- `Command::id` (the action name — the label carries the owning component's title and changes with mounting) and `Command::group`, both optional.\n- `WidgetIntent::MenuHistoryUp`/`Down` → `menu_history_up`/`down` in `[keys.widgets]`, bound to `Shift+Arrows` and `Ctrl+p`/`Ctrl+n`. Every key first proposed was already `menu_up`/`menu_down`; these were checked free against the config and against `Input`'s own key handling.\n- Host: `AppState.search_store` (in-memory until T386), `SearchModel::new(\"command\", …)`, `.id(row.id)`, `.group(group_of(...))`.\n\n**The ordering rule became `Command::group`, applied only while the query is empty.** The host could not do it: it builds the row list once at open time and the query changes later inside the widget, so a query-dependent sort cannot live there. Group 0 is the focused component's actions; the widget drops the grouping the moment anything is typed.\n\n**A bug caught in review, not by tests:** the recall path first called `on_query_changed()`, which resets the history cursor — so a second `Ctrl+p` would have restarted from the newest and history would have been one step deep. Typing and recall are separate hooks now (`on_query_changed` vs `on_query_replaced`), and the test walks two steps back specifically to catch it.\n\n**A rendering bug I introduced and the user caught on screen.** `Label` marks were drawn by splitting the line into runs at the mark boundaries; the shaper drops a run's **leading whitespace**, so every run after the first landed a cell early and the spaces walked — `Toggle Left Sidebar` drew as `ToggleLe ftSidebar`. Scene-level tests could not see it: the runs they assert were correct and the loss happened in shaping. The line is drawn whole now, with marked characters over-drawn per character — which is what the palette did before this phase, and has no run boundary to break.\n\nFollow-ups that came out of using it, both done: the frecency recalibration (T384) and `MatchCase` + `[settings] search_case` (T389).",
      "notes": "",
