      "startedAt": "2026-08-04T09:01:55.909Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-08-04T13:10:17.162Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-03T16:19:00.399Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T13:10:17.162Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1906a6c-7eb4-434f-b603-61aed6117696",
      "phaseId": "9a4fa5f5-62a8-4243-85ab-de8edaed21e8",
      "number": 386,
      "shortId": "KA7ZC",
      "priority": 3,
      "shortName": "the-app-remembers-it-the-first",
      "title": "The app remembers it: the first persisted runtime state — versioned, bounded, corrupt-safe",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "A history that dies with the process is not a history. This task gives the app its **first piece of persisted runtime state**, and defines the shape once so the next feature that needs it does not invent a second one.\n\n## There is no precedent — that is the point\n\n`heca-config` only ever reads: `heca-config/src/loader.rs:238/246` resolve `~/.config/heca/config.toml` and `keybindings.toml`, `:320` uses `dirs::config_dir()`, and `heca-theme/src/loader.rs:10` does the same. The only `fs::write` calls outside tests are `heca/src/app/backend_factory.rs:276` and terminal plumbing. **Nothing the user does survives a restart today.**\n\nSo this does not go in `heca-config`: that crate is the user's hand-written configuration, and machine-written state has different rules (it is rewritten without asking, it may be deleted safely, and a corrupt one must never stop the app). New module `heca/src/search_state.rs`, owned by the app that owns `AppState`.\n\n## Location and format\n\n`dirs::data_dir()/heca/search-history.toml` — `data_dir`, not `config_dir`: `~/.local/share/heca` on Linux, `~/Library/Application Support/heca` on macOS. Deleting it must lose nothing but convenience.\n\nTOML, to avoid adding a serialization dependency for one small file (**check `Cargo.toml` first** — if `serde_json` is already in the tree, JSON is the better fit for machine-written data and the argument for TOML disappears). Either way it carries `version = 1` from the first line written: a `@`/`:` scope arriving later must be able to read or discard an older file deliberately rather than by accident.\n\nThe snapshot is `SearchStore` whole (see T384) — the per-scope histories, the frecency counts, and the monotonic `seq`, which **must** be persisted or every restart resets recency to \"everything is equally old\".\n\n## Behaviour\n\n- **Load once at startup**, into `AppState`. A missing file is an empty store; a corrupt or unreadable one is *also* an empty store, logged once at warn — never a startup failure, and never a dialog. The palette must work identically on a first run.\n- **Save after a run records something.** The file is a few KB; there is no need for a background writer or a debounce, and saving at exit alone loses everything on a crash or a `kill`.\n- **Atomic**: write a sibling temp file, then rename. A half-written history read at next launch is the corrupt case above, but there is no reason to manufacture it.\n- **Bounded on write** as well as in memory (~50 queries per scope, ~500 frecency ids) so the file cannot grow unbounded no matter what the in-memory caps do.\n\n## Settings\n\n`[settings] search_history = true` to turn the memory off entirely — a shared or recorded machine is a real reason, and a user who turns it off expects the existing file to stop being written *and* to stop being read. `heca_config::settings::PaletteSize` (used at `heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:190`) is the precedent for adding a setting and threading it into `AppState`.\n\n## `clear_search_history` — a real action, the full checklist\n\nUnlike `menu_history_up/down` (widget intents, T385), clearing the store **is** an app action: it changes persisted state and a user wants it from the palette and from RPC. It therefore goes through the whole Adding-New-Actions checklist in AGENTS.md — `WmAction` variant, name parsing (`heca/src/input.rs:685` is the pattern), registry handler (`heca/src/app/registry.rs:999`), `ActionPolicy`, `ActionMeta` with label/description/icon/args, RPC name (`heca/src/rpc.rs:617`) — and it takes no arguments, so it is offerable in the palette (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:138`). Ask before adding a confirm prompt; it is destructive but trivially rebuilt by using the app.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- Two windows / two processes writing the same file: last writer wins, and the atomic rename keeps the file valid. Do not build locking for this — say plainly in the module doc that it is last-writer-wins, so nobody later assumes merging.\n- A file from a future `version` is discarded, not parsed leniently.\n- `dirs::data_dir()` returning `None` disables persistence for the session — in memory only, no panic.\n- The store is written from the UI thread: keep it that way and keep it small, rather than introducing a thread for a few KB.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Round-trip through a temp dir: record, save, load, and the ordering that comes back is identical.\n- A corrupt file, a truncated file and a future `version` each yield an empty store and no error out of `load`.\n- The caps hold on write: recording 200 queries writes 50.\n- `search_history = false` neither reads nor writes.\n- No test may touch the real `dirs::data_dir()` — the path is a parameter of the loader, exactly so.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone. `heca/src/search_state.rs` — the first runtime state this app keeps across restarts. Verified in the app by the user 2026-08-04 (\"works fine\"), before the save trigger was reworked (see below).\n\n**JSON, not TOML** — the task said to check first, and `serde_json` was already a dependency of `heca`, so the argument for TOML disappeared. `dirs::data_dir()/heca/search-history.json`; `data_dir`, not `config_dir`, because this is not something a user writes.\n\n- **`load` cannot fail.** Missing, unreadable, malformed, or a version this build does not know all give an empty store. A search history is not worth a failed launch.\n- **Atomic write** — sibling temp file then rename.\n- **`seq` is persisted with the counts**, deliberately: recency is a distance in the use-sequence rather than a timestamp, so dropping it would make everything equally old on the next launch — the exact bug this task fixes.\n- `[settings] search_history` (off ⇒ neither written nor read), `search_history_size` (50) and `search_usage_size` (500), all documented in `config.default.toml`. The caps are `search::Caps`, applied in memory *and* on write.\n\n**The save is not the palette's, at the user's instruction.** `SearchStore::revision()` bumps on every `record_run`; `search_state::persist_if_changed(state)` writes only when it moved, comparing against `AppState.search_saved_revision`. A consumer records and does nothing else — it cannot forget to persist, because recording *is* the request. Making every consumer call save would mean the second search surface silently stops being remembered, with nothing to report it. `save` stays public for a forced flush.\n\nDocumented in `docs/widgets.md` (Foundations → Search), `AGENTS.md` (a STOP-section rule: one matcher in the workspace, never call save, pass your own scope) and `README.md` (human-facing).\n\n**Two things left open on the phase, both raised by the user and neither in this task:**\n1. **Multi-process is last-writer-wins.** Two heca instances overwrite each other's history. The fix is neovim-shada-style merge-on-write, which needs a globally comparable ordering — the app must stamp wall-clock times when persisting and rebuild the sequence from them on load, keeping the library clock-free. This changes the file schema and should land before anyone has a file worth keeping.\n2. **`clear_search_history`** — needs a decision on one verb clearing both queries and usage per scope, or two. `SearchStore::clear_scope` / `clear` already exist for it.\n\n**Verification gap to be honest about:** the user verified persistence when the save was called directly from the palette's resolve path. It is now driven by the revision check. The behaviour is the same by construction and covered by tests, but the reworked path has not been driven in the app.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "2026-08-04T13:22:34.972Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-08-04T13:57:41.161Z",
