    {
      "id": "63b4db04-4db5-4ffc-8a75-b8e8d24398bc",
      "phaseId": "9a4fa5f5-62a8-4243-85ab-de8edaed21e8",
      "number": 391,
      "shortId": "J7SAP",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "two-heca-instances-share-one-h",
      "title": "Two heca instances share one history instead of overwriting it — merge on write, shada-style",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "Raised by the user 2026-08-04: running several heca instances is normal, and the second one to save currently discards the first's history. T386 shipped last-writer-wins and wrote that into the module docs as if it were acceptable. It is not.\n\n## The obstacle, and why it is not a small patch\n\nA merge needs an ordering both processes agree on, and the library's is **process-local by design**: recency in `heca-grid-ui/src/search.rs` is a distance in a use-sequence, not a timestamp, so a UI library needs no clock and its tests assert exact numbers. Instance A's \"seq 40\" and instance B's \"seq 12\" have no relationship, so two files cannot be ordered against each other.\n\n## The resolution — the clock lives in the app\n\n`heca/src/search_state.rs` already implies a filesystem, so it may have a clock. The library stays clock-free.\n\n1. **Schema v2.** Entries carry wall-clock seconds: `PersistedUse { id, count, last_used_at }` and `PersistedQuery { query, at }`. The process-local `seq` leaves the file. A v1 file is discarded by the existing version check — which is why this should land before anyone has a history worth keeping.\n2. **On load, rebuild the sequence** from the timestamps: entries sorted oldest-first take positions `1..n`. `Frecency::restore` then receives exactly what it would have built itself.\n3. **On save, merge by delta.** Re-read the file (another instance may have written since we loaded), and for each id apply *what we added since our baseline* on top of the disk's count. Two instances that each ran a command twice from a count of 5 must end at **9**. A plain `max` silently drops one of them, which is the bug in a subtler form.\n4. **A `Baseline`** — what this instance last agreed the file said — is what makes a delta computable at all: without it a merge cannot tell \"the disk says 5 because of us\" from \"the disk says 5 because of another window\". Held on `AppState`, returned by `load` and by each `save`.\n5. **Queries merge as a union**, the newer timestamp winning, then capped.\n\n## Not solved, and say so\n\nTwo saves landing at the same instant can still lose one increment — this is a read-modify-write with no lock. **Neovim has the same race and accepts it.** Closing it means advisory file locking: a dependency and a new failure mode, in exchange for one increment. Record the decision in the module docs rather than leaving the reader to wonder.\n\n## Test\n\nThe one that matters: two stores loaded from the same file, each recording the same id twice and its own distinct query, saved in turn — the count is the **sum**, and both queries survive. Plus: a save that recorded nothing must not inflate what is already there (the delta being zero), and a v1 file is discarded rather than read leniently.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone. Schema **v2** in `heca/src/search_state.rs`: entries carry wall-clock seconds (`last_used_at`, and `at` per query) and the process-local `seq` leaves the file.\n\n- **On load the sequence is rebuilt** from the timestamps — entries sorted oldest-first take positions `1..n` — so `heca-grid-ui` still receives exactly what it would have built itself and stays clock-free. That was the obstacle: recency in the library is a distance in a use-sequence, so instance A's \"seq 40\" and B's \"seq 12\" were incomparable.\n- **On save, counts merge by delta**: re-read the file (another instance may have written since we loaded), then apply what *this* instance added since its `Baseline` on top of the disk's count. Two instances each running a command twice from a count of 5 end at **9**. A plain `max` would silently drop one side — the same bug in a subtler form.\n- `Baseline` (returned by `load` and by each `save`, held on `AppState`) is what makes a delta computable: without it a merge cannot tell \"the disk says 5 because of us\" from \"because of another window\".\n- Queries merge as a union, the newer timestamp winning, then capped.\n\n**Not solved, and written into the module docs:** two saves landing at the same instant can still lose one increment — a read-modify-write with no lock. Neovim has the same race and accepts it; closing it means advisory locking, a dependency and a new failure mode, for one increment.\n\nThe decisive test is `two_instances_merge_instead_of_overwriting`: two stores loaded from one file, each recording the same id twice and its own query, saved in turn — the count is the sum and both queries survive. `saving_twice_without_recording_changes_nothing` guards the delta being zero, and a v1 file is discarded by the version check rather than read leniently.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "2026-08-04T14:03:25.243Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-08-04T14:17:28.917Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-04T14:03:21.821Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T14:17:28.917Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "15f91cc6-9dd1-4c04-b533-c51f9eace1d3",
      "phaseId": "9a4fa5f5-62a8-4243-85ab-de8edaed21e8",
      "number": 392,
      "shortId": "A5DQT",
      "priority": 6,
      "shortName": "forgetting-clear-search-histor",
      "title": "Forgetting: clear_search_history and clear_search_ranking, per scope",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "Requested by the user 2026-08-04. **Done in the same session.**\n\n## Two verbs, not one\n\nClearing what you typed and clearing what you use are different intentions, and someone asking for one rarely means the other. `clear_search_history` forgets past queries; `clear_search_ranking` forgets the usage counts that order a list.\n\nBoth take an **optional `scope`**: bare forgets every search surface — what a key or a palette entry means — and a caller that knows which surface it wants names it. Optional is what keeps them offerable in the palette at all; a required argument is filtered out by the arity rule (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs::offerable`).\n\n## The trap this uncovered\n\n**Clearing cannot simply empty the store.** The next save *merges* with what is on disk (T391), so an emptied store would have every entry pulled straight back in on the following write. Clearing is the one operation that must **remove** rather than combine, so `search_state::forget` rewrites the file — stripping only the half it was asked for — and resets the baseline to what it wrote.\n\n`forget_on_disk` is split out from `forget` for exactly one reason: `forget` takes `&mut AppState`, which cannot exist in a test (it needs a window). The file half takes a path and is tested directly.\n\n## Where it landed\n\nFull Adding-New-Actions checklist: `WmAction::ClearSearchHistory`/`ClearSearchRanking` (`heca/src/input.rs`), bare and argument name parsing, `action_priority`, `ActionPolicy::Global` (`heca/src/app/interaction.rs` — forgetting a memory touches no layout, so no domain should refuse it), handlers (`heca/src/handlers.rs`), registry (`heca/src/app/registry.rs`), `ActionDescriptor`s with the optional arg (`heca/src/actions.rs`), and RPC `clear-search-history` / `clear-search-ranking` (`heca/src/rpc.rs`).\n\nLibrary side: `SearchStore::clear_history(Option<&str>)` and `clear_ranking(Option<&str>)`, each returning whether anything was there to forget and bumping the revision.\n\nNo confirm prompt was added — it is destructive but trivially rebuilt by using the app. Ask before adding one.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone — see the description. Tests: `forgetting_queries_leaves_the_ranking_alone` (the queries go, the ranking stays, and an emptied scope is dropped from the file entirely), `forgetting_one_scope_leaves_the_others`, and `queries_and_ranking_are_cleared_independently` in the library. Documented in `README.md`; the scope recipe and a worked end-to-end consumer example are in `docs/widgets.md`.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "2026-08-04T14:17:54.398Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-08-04T14:17:54.398Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-04T14:17:47.572Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T14:17:54.398Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "3f7db50c-c9a4-446b-8cf1-d3292a32200c",
      "phaseId": "9a4fa5f5-62a8-4243-85ab-de8edaed21e8",
      "number": 393,
      "shortId": "5ZUQ2",
      "priority": 7,
      "shortName": "the-palette-opens-in-a-scope-p",
      "title": "The palette opens in a scope — pane and workspace pickers on their own keys",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "Asked for by the user 2026-08-04: `prefix+p` actions, `prefix+o` pane search, `prefix+O` workspaces — one surface, opened against a chosen mode, each remembering separately.\n\n**Everything underneath supports it. Nothing switches mode at runtime yet.**\n\n## ✅ Settled with the user — do not re-open\n\n| Decision | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Sigils | `:` actions (the default), `@` panes, `$` workspaces |\n| Action | **`command_palette` gains two optional args**: `mode` and `query` |\n| Default | no args ⇒ actions, empty query — exactly today's behaviour |\n| Pane rank id | its **display name** (`custom_name` if set, else `title`) |\n| Workspace rank id | its **`name`** when set; **no id at all** when unnamed |\n| Auto-submit | **No.** Rejected deliberately — see below |\n| Ranking ephemeral rows | **Yes**, it is safe — see below |\n\n**Why no auto-submit.** It would run whichever row ranked first, and ranking moves with the user's habits — so the same binding could do something different next month, silently. A keybinding is the one place unpredictability is unacceptable. If the target is known that reliably, bind the real action or a `focus_pane name=…`.\n\n**Why ranking ephemeral rows is safe.** The usage table caps at 500 per scope and prunes the lowest-scoring, so dead names age out on their own; nothing else reads those ids, so a key that never matches again is inert. The value is real: you jump to the same few panes constantly and they have distinctive names. Where it degrades it degrades gently — five panes titled `zsh` share one entry, which boosts all of them equally. Useless, not wrong. A rename resets that row's learning and it re-learns in a few uses.\n\n**Why not `PaneId` / `WorkspaceId`.** `PaneId(u64)` is minted from `Session.next_id`, which **restarts at 1 every launch** (`heca-core/src/layout/session.rs:81`), so tomorrow's `PaneId(3)` is a different pane. A workspace *index* is worse: indices are reused, so a persisted count would attach itself to whatever workspace later takes that slot — actively wrong rather than merely useless.\n\n## What exists already\n\n- `SearchModel::with_scope(scope)` — **added this session, uncommitted at the time of writing**: same store and same case preference, different scope. This is what lets one palette hold three memories.\n- `CommandPalette::search(model)` already takes the model, so the palette is scope-agnostic by construction.\n- `Command::id` (the rank key) and `Command::group` exist.\n- The clear actions already address a scope by name (`clear_search_history scope=pane`).\n- Rows and their intents: `heca/src/chrome/palette.rs::entries` builds `PaletteEntry { id, label, description, icon, shortcuts, intent }` from the `ActionCatalog`, and `on_resolve` looks the chosen row up by `id` and dispatches its `intent`.\n\n## The build, in order\n\n### 1. Widget — modes selected by a sigil (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/command_palette.rs`)\n\n**Keep one flat `commands` list.** Each `Command` declares which mode it belongs to; the active mode is a *filter*, not a second list. This matters: the row text children are built one per command in `command()` and indexed by position, so separate per-mode lists would mean rebuilding or re-indexing children on every mode switch.\n\n```rust\nCommandPalette::new()\n    .mode('@', \"pane\")            // sigil → scope name\n    .mode('$', \"workspace\")\n    .command(Command::new(\"Close Pane\", …).id(\"close\").mode(':'))\n    .command(Command::new(\"nvim\", …).id(\"nvim\").mode('@'))\n```\n\n- The **default mode** is the one with no sigil typed. `:` is its sigil so it can be returned to explicitly.\n- `active_mode()` reads the **first character of the query**; `effective_query()` is the rest. Everything downstream — `rank`, the marks, `record_run` — uses the effective query, never the raw one.\n- The model for the active mode is `self.search.with_scope(scope)`. One `SearchModel` is handed in; the others are derived.\n- `run_selected` records into the **active mode's** scope, with the effective query.\n\nEdge cases to get right: a lone sigil (`@` with nothing after) lists that mode unfiltered; an unknown leading character is *not* a sigil and is matched literally; switching mode resets `selected`/`scroll` and re-marks; the history keys walk the **active mode's** history, so `Ctrl+p` in `@` mode must not offer command queries.\n\n### 2. App — the row sources (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs`)\n\n`open_command_palette` has one hardcoded source. It needs three, each tagged with its mode, and `PaletteEntry` needs a **second id**:\n\n- `id` — unique across all modes for the overlay round-trip (`\"pane:3\"`, `\"ws:0\"`);\n- `rank_id` — what frecency counts, per the table above (the display name), passed as `Command::id`.\n\nThey are the same string for actions today and must stop being assumed identical.\n\n**Where the rows come from:** `AppState.session.workspaces` → `ws.scrolling.columns` → `col.panes`. `heca/src/providers/workspaces/model.rs:185-235` already walks exactly this and is the naming precedent — `ws.name.unwrap_or_else(|| format!(\"Workspace {}\", ws_idx + 1))`, pane `custom_name` over `title`. Follow it rather than inventing a second convention.\n\n**What running a row does:** actions keep their current intent; a pane row dispatches `WmAction::FocusPane { pane_id }` (`heca/src/input.rs:135`); a workspace row `WmAction::FocusWorkspace { ws_idx }` (`:138`).\n\n### 3. The action (full Adding-New-Actions checklist)\n\n`WmAction::CommandPalette` is a **unit variant** today (`heca/src/input.rs:360`) with `args: &[]` (`heca/src/actions.rs:1300`). It becomes:\n\n```rust\nCommandPalette { mode: Option<String>, query: Option<String> }\n```\n\n**Both optional** — a required argument would be filtered out of the palette by the arity rule, and bare must stay exactly what it is now. Sites: the variant, bare name parsing (`input.rs:686` area), the argument form (`input.rs:965` area), `action_priority` (`input.rs:~1128`), `ActionPolicy` (it is `AlwaysAllowed` today — keep it), the descriptor's `args`, the registry handler, and RPC (`rpc.rs`).\n\n**`mode` and `query` compose into one prefilled query** — `mode=pane query=nvim` opens with `\"@nvim\"`. One mechanism, not two: the sigil *is* the mode selector, so the argument only has to build the initial string. That also means the widget needs no \"open in mode\" API at all.\n\nA prefilled query that runs something **is recorded in history** as if typed — it is the query that found the thing, and the consecutive-repeat dedupe means a binding pressed twenty times leaves one entry.\n\n### 4. Bindings\n\n`keybindings.default.toml`: `prefix+p` → `command_palette`, `prefix+o` → `command_palette mode=pane`, `prefix+O` → `command_palette mode=workspace`. Check the parameterized-binding syntax that `spawn_command` uses — the contract is described in AGENTS.md under \"Planned parameterized binding contract\".\n\n## Tests\n\n- Typing `@` switches the list to panes and does **not** filter them by the sigil character.\n- The three modes keep separate histories: record in `@`, then `Ctrl+p` in the default mode offers nothing.\n- The marks land correctly on a filtered mode — the effective query, not the raw one, is what was matched.\n- A pane row runs `FocusPane` with the right id; a workspace row `FocusWorkspace`.\n- Bare `command_palette` behaves exactly as before (the regression guard that matters).\n\n**Visual verification is the user's** — every mode switch is a visible reflow.\n\n## Relationship to T390\n\n`P028(F004)/T390` extracts the picker out of `CommandPalette` so it can be mounted anywhere. Either order works, but **not both at once** — they touch the same file. Doing this first gives the extraction a second and third real caller to justify its shape.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone 2026-08-04. One palette, three lists, reached by a sigil typed as the query's first character.\n\n## What shipped\n\n**Widget** (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/command_palette.rs`)\n- `CommandPalette::mode(sigil, scope)` — a sigil→scope table; the **first declared is the default**. No modes declared = the palette that existed before (one scope, no sigil).\n- `Command::mode(scope)` — takes the **scope name, not the sigil char**. `char` is not an accepted `#[prop]` type (it falls to the `_` arm, which needs `PropName`), and the scope name is already the vocabulary of `clear_search_history scope=…`. The sigil stays a palette-level input convention.\n- `active()` returns `(Option<char> sigil, scope, effective query)`. Everything downstream — rank, marks, `record_run`, history — uses the effective query. `results()` filters by mode, ranks over the filtered set, then **maps indices back into the full `commands` list** (children, marks and measured heights are keyed by position).\n- `two_line_rows()` and `shortcut_col_w()` are now the **active mode's**, not the whole list's.\n- History is per mode; entries are stored without the sigil and the sigil is put back on recall. `on_query_changed` resets the walk in **every** scope (one field, one walk).\n- `CommandPalette::query(text)` — the prefill; also syncs marks/wrap/selection so it is order-independent with `open`.\n- `label_signals()` + ranking off the **live** label text, so a row renamed while open is both redrawn and matched by its new name.\n- `Command::current(bool)` — the row you are on, drawn in the theme accent (icon + title); selection still wins on its own row.\n- `Command::preselect(bool)` + `sync_initial_selection()` — selection starts there when nothing is typed; a query hands the lead back to the best match.\n\n**App** (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs`)\n- `pane_entries(session, programs, last_focused)` / `workspace_entries(session, last_visited)`, both pure.\n- `PaletteEntry` gained `rank_id` (split from `id`), `mode`, `pane`, `current`, `preselect`.\n- Rank ids are **names**: pane display name; workspace `name` only when set (`None` when unnamed).\n- `sigil_for(mode)`; `open_command_palette(state, mode, query)` composes them into one prefilled query.\n- One `create_effect` per pane row over the chrome store's existing `program`/`custom_name` signals → live labels, no rebuild, no flash.\n\n**Action** — `WmAction::CommandPalette { mode: Option<String>, query: Option<String> }`, both optional, full checklist: variant, bare parse, arg parse, `action_priority`, `ActionPolicy` (unchanged, `AlwaysAllowed`), descriptor args, handler, RPC + its doc header.\n\n**Config** — the global `[[keys.bind]]` form **did not exist** (only `[[keys.component.bind]]`). Added `KeysConfig::bind: Vec<ModeBindingConfig>`, merged **by combo** (not replaced wholesale) and bound through the same `action_ref_from_config` seam in `build_keymap`.\n\n## Decisions taken here, not in the original spec\n\n- **`prefix+O` is unavailable.** `KeyCombo::parse` lowercases character keys, so `prefix+O` *is* `prefix+o` (the pane picker), and `prefix+Shift+o` is `follow_link`. Antonio chose **`prefix+Ctrl+o`** for workspaces.\n- **A pane's name is not `Pane::title`.** That field is empty in a running app; the name on screen comes from `pane_info_view`, which resolves the live `PaneRuntime.program` through `ProgramsConfig` with `custom_name` overriding. Reading the field directly produced blank rows while the sidebar read `zsh`. The pane row description leads with the working directory (`~/… · Workspace 1`) so several `zsh` rows are tellable apart.\n- **Preselect is the LAST pane/workspace, not the current one** (`state.last_focused`, `state.last_visited_ws_idx`): landing on the current one would make Enter re-focus what is already focused. The accent marks the current one instead.\n- Floating panes are not listed — `floating_panes` is still marked \"future feature\" in `heca-core`.\n\n## Verification\n\n1150 tests, clippy 0 warnings. New tests: sigil switching without self-filtering, per-mode history + sigil restored on recall, marks on the effective query, a mode-less palette unchanged, live label redrawn *and* matched, preselect until typed, pane rank id from the live runtime with an empty `title`, only-a-named-workspace ranks, mode+query composition, and `command_palette` still bare-bindable/offerable.\n\n**Not verified visually** — Antonio drove the app and reported the blank pane names (fixed). The accent on the current row, the live rename redraw, and opening already-scrolled to a preselected row still need eyes.\n\n## Not a defect\n\n\"The app does not start\" was traced to `cargo watch`: `.planner/` is tracked, so planner writes retrigger it, and concurrent `cargo test`/`clippy` runs held the build lock. Launched directly the binary starts instantly. Suggested `cargo watch --ignore '.planner/**'` or `-w <src dirs>`.",
